1
00:00:02,794 --> 00:00:04,213
What are you drawing over there?

2
00:00:04,421 --> 00:00:08,592
A hypothetical containment field
for a Frisbee-sized wormhole...

3
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:11,470
...that could serve as a portal
to a parallel universe.

4
00:00:12,095 --> 00:00:14,890
Oh, you silly doodlebug.

5
00:00:16,266 --> 00:00:19,269
A lot of scientists believe
making contact with other life-forms...

6
00:00:19,478 --> 00:00:21,647
...would probably not end well for us.

7
00:00:21,855 --> 00:00:23,690
It's a Frisbee-sized wormhole, Leonard.

8
00:00:23,899 --> 00:00:26,151
You could block it with a Frisbee.
Calm down.

9
00:00:29,780 --> 00:00:31,782
Do you expect me to build this? Heh.

10
00:00:31,990 --> 00:00:35,244
I expect you to wipe the pudding
off your chin. Gentlemen.

11
00:00:37,162 --> 00:00:41,166
Have you guys ever noticed that Sheldon
always disappears every day at 2:45?

12
00:00:41,375 --> 00:00:43,585
-Really?
-He probably just goes to the bathroom.

13
00:00:43,794 --> 00:00:46,547
Actually, no. He goes at 8 a.m.
with optional follow-ups...

14
00:00:46,755 --> 00:00:50,676
...at 1:45 and 7:10 on high-fiber Fridays.

15
00:00:51,843 --> 00:00:53,428
It's sad that you know that.

16
00:00:53,637 --> 00:00:56,515
Oh....
That's just the tip of the sadness iceberg.

17
00:00:58,684 --> 00:01:00,644
I'm looking at his public calendar.

18
00:01:00,852 --> 00:01:03,730
Two-forty-five to 3:05, nothing.

19
00:01:03,939 --> 00:01:06,191
Yesterday, 2:45 to 3:05, nothing.

20
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:08,944
Last week, nothing. Last month, nothing.

21
00:01:09,152 --> 00:01:11,697
He never has anything booked
during that time.

22
00:01:11,905 --> 00:01:14,700
Twenty minutes a day
completely unaccounted for.

23
00:01:14,908 --> 00:01:18,537
-We should figure out where he goes.
-Ooh, this is exciting.

24
00:01:19,705 --> 00:01:22,499
Like one of my classic
murder-mystery dinner parties.

25
00:01:22,708 --> 00:01:27,838
Right, the case of who murdered
three Saturday nights of my life.

26
00:01:28,046 --> 00:01:31,341
Colonel Koothrappali in the kitchen
with the olive spread.

27
00:01:32,884 --> 00:01:35,929
It was tapenade and you guys suck.

28
00:02:00,787 --> 00:02:01,872
So how was work today?

29
00:02:02,414 --> 00:02:03,498
The 43 Peculiarity

30
00:02:03,665 --> 00:02:05,417
Well, I spent most of the afternoon
growing isotopically pure crystals...

31
00:02:05,626 --> 00:02:07,628
...for neutrino detection.

32
00:02:09,421 --> 00:02:11,423
That sounds like fun.

33
00:02:12,132 --> 00:02:15,260
-Yeah, it was.
-Oh, good, I guessed right.

34
00:02:16,094 --> 00:02:17,095
[PHONE BEEPS]

35
00:02:17,304 --> 00:02:19,973
-Who you talking to?
-Oh, just this guy I met at school.

36
00:02:20,182 --> 00:02:22,934
Oh, great. We're still dating, right?

37
00:02:24,102 --> 00:02:28,106
Relax, he's just a friend. We're doing
an oral report together. He's really nice.

38
00:02:28,315 --> 00:02:31,652
I'm sorry, what was that?
I had a little stroke after "oral."

39
00:02:32,527 --> 00:02:33,862
Would you stop?

40
00:02:34,071 --> 00:02:35,113
[PHONE BEEPS]

41
00:02:35,322 --> 00:02:37,616
He moved here from London.
He doesn't know anybody.

42
00:02:37,824 --> 00:02:41,620
Oh, good, an English accent,
the sexiest accent you can have.

43
00:02:41,828 --> 00:02:43,330
No, that's not true.

44
00:02:43,538 --> 00:02:46,333
There's French, there's Italian.

45
00:02:46,541 --> 00:02:48,835
No, you're right, it's the best.

46
00:02:49,670 --> 00:02:52,297
-Did you tell him you have a boyfriend?
-It didn't come up.

47
00:02:52,506 --> 00:02:54,007
Maybe you should tell him.

48
00:02:54,216 --> 00:02:55,801
What am I supposed to say?

49
00:02:56,009 --> 00:02:58,470
Say, "Can't talk right now.
Hanging with my boyfriend.

50
00:02:58,679 --> 00:03:01,473
England sucks, you suck,
U.S.A. number one."

51
00:03:04,810 --> 00:03:09,606
Fine. "Hanging with my boyfriend.
Talk to you later."

52
00:03:09,815 --> 00:03:11,233
-Happy?
-Yes. Thank you.

53
00:03:11,441 --> 00:03:12,484
PENNY:
Mm-hm.

54
00:03:12,693 --> 00:03:14,027
[PHONE BEEPS]

55
00:03:14,695 --> 00:03:16,279
Huh.

56
00:03:18,949 --> 00:03:21,159
-What did he say?
-Nothing.

57
00:03:21,368 --> 00:03:22,452
What?

58
00:03:23,078 --> 00:03:25,455
"Did your boyfriend make you type that?"

59
00:03:33,588 --> 00:03:34,673
I hate this guy.

60
00:03:37,884 --> 00:03:41,263
-Don't be like that.
-Oh, come on. Trust me, he's hitting on you.

61
00:03:41,471 --> 00:03:44,057
He's not, we're just friends.
Is this gonna be a problem?

62
00:03:44,266 --> 00:03:46,518
He's supposed to come over
tomorrow to work.

63
00:03:46,727 --> 00:03:47,853
Really? Here?

64
00:03:48,061 --> 00:03:51,857
If it makes you uncomfortable, I'll switch
partners even though the thing's due...

65
00:03:52,065 --> 00:03:55,986
...and everyone already has a partner
and I'll probably end up failing the class.

66
00:03:56,194 --> 00:03:57,487
That'd be great. Thank you.

67
00:04:02,701 --> 00:04:04,369
Two forty-four. Right on schedule.

68
00:04:04,578 --> 00:04:05,871
Hey, Sheldon.

69
00:04:06,079 --> 00:04:07,831
Oh, hello.

70
00:04:08,039 --> 00:04:12,210
Raj and I are heading over to the genetics lab
to pet the glow-in-the-dark bunny.

71
00:04:13,253 --> 00:04:15,005
-Wanna come with us?
-No, thank you.

72
00:04:15,213 --> 00:04:20,093
You sure? They turn off the lights and it's
like a little laser show that poops all over.

73
00:04:20,927 --> 00:04:22,512
I'm quite sure. Good day.

74
00:04:22,721 --> 00:04:24,723
-Where are you going?
-Where are you going?

75
00:04:24,973 --> 00:04:26,558
-We told you.
-Yeah, I just told you.

76
00:04:26,767 --> 00:04:29,770
-No, you didn't.
-Your word against mine. See you in court.

77
00:04:33,272 --> 00:04:35,817
-Should we follow him?
-I don't know. I'm torn.

78
00:04:36,026 --> 00:04:39,613
I want to know where he's going but
now I kind of want to play with the bunny.

79
00:04:42,282 --> 00:04:43,408
-Hey, guys.
-Hey, Alex.

80
00:04:43,617 --> 00:04:45,577
-Do you know where your boss just went?
-No.

81
00:04:45,786 --> 00:04:48,330
-Don't you know his schedule?
-All I know is corduroy...

82
00:04:48,538 --> 00:04:51,416
...makes too much noise
and I have to go find quieter pants.

83
00:04:56,129 --> 00:04:57,214
Come on.

84
00:04:57,422 --> 00:05:01,927
Boy, what I wouldn't give
to get her out of those pants.

85
00:05:02,135 --> 00:05:05,096
And into something a little more stylish.

86
00:05:19,318 --> 00:05:21,279
This is where he goes?

87
00:05:21,488 --> 00:05:24,115
-What's in there?
-I think it's an old storage room.

88
00:05:24,699 --> 00:05:28,620
What could he be doing in there every day
for 20 minutes?

89
00:05:28,829 --> 00:05:30,497
Well, he's not doing 20-minute abs...

90
00:05:30,705 --> 00:05:33,500
...because if he were,
he'd have way better abs.

91
00:05:37,504 --> 00:05:40,339
-Can you hear anything?
-Not yet.

92
00:05:52,894 --> 00:05:54,771
What are you doing?

93
00:05:54,980 --> 00:05:57,148
I'm listening.

94
00:05:57,356 --> 00:06:00,402
Can't you face the other way and listen?

95
00:06:00,610 --> 00:06:03,530
I can't do anything right for you, can I?

96
00:06:04,197 --> 00:06:05,699
What the hell is he up to?

97
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:08,285
He is kind of a weirdo.

98
00:06:08,493 --> 00:06:11,621
Maybe he's got Leonard Nimoy
chained up in there.

99
00:06:11,830 --> 00:06:14,624
Or Bill Gates. Or Stephen Hawking.

100
00:06:15,374 --> 00:06:18,377
Why would he chain up Stephen Hawking?

101
00:06:19,254 --> 00:06:23,842
Howard, you can't treat the man differently
just because he's disabled. That's not okay.

102
00:06:28,387 --> 00:06:30,515
If you're wondering why I've been staring...

103
00:06:30,724 --> 00:06:33,894
...I'm trying to get a look at this guy
who's coming over to Penny's.

104
00:06:34,436 --> 00:06:37,022
To be honest, I didn't know you were here.

105
00:06:39,274 --> 00:06:41,651
It's not a big deal.
He's just in her history class.

106
00:06:41,860 --> 00:06:44,529
They're working on a project.
I don't even know why I care.

107
00:06:44,738 --> 00:06:48,241
-You know what, I don't care.
-You think you don't care.

108
00:06:50,076 --> 00:06:52,829
This is silly. I have nothing to worry about.

109
00:06:53,038 --> 00:06:55,248
Oh, I don't know.

110
00:06:56,583 --> 00:07:00,378
Hey, statistically speaking, I'm sure
you have something to worry about.

111
00:07:01,588 --> 00:07:02,589
What do you mean?

112
00:07:02,797 --> 00:07:06,843
Well, if we assume
that your looks are average...

113
00:07:07,052 --> 00:07:10,722
...right off the bat, 50 percent of men on
Earth are more attractive than you.

114
00:07:13,183 --> 00:07:18,813
That's 1.5 billion handsome lads
standing by, waiting to rain on your parade.

115
00:07:20,774 --> 00:07:23,068
Well, yeah, but this isn't just about looks.

116
00:07:23,276 --> 00:07:26,112
I'm way above average
in a lot of other things.

117
00:07:26,321 --> 00:07:31,785
Not height, vision, athleticism,
or ability to digest dairy products.

118
00:07:32,786 --> 00:07:36,414
I'm talking about important things
like emotional maturity.

119
00:07:36,623 --> 00:07:40,335
You were just spying on your girlfriend
through a peephole.

120
00:07:41,586 --> 00:07:44,506
I liked it better
when you thought I wasn't here.

121
00:07:44,714 --> 00:07:47,384
I'm not saying
you don't have attractive qualities.

122
00:07:47,592 --> 00:07:50,095
Your choice of friends is impeccable.

123
00:07:50,303 --> 00:07:51,763
You're a good sleeper.

124
00:07:52,931 --> 00:07:57,310
And last but not least,
you buy the grapes I like.

125
00:07:59,145 --> 00:08:01,439
You're a real catch compared
to some snoring guy...

126
00:08:01,648 --> 00:08:04,526
...with a fridge full of lousy grapes.

127
00:08:05,777 --> 00:08:06,778
None of this matters.

128
00:08:06,987 --> 00:08:10,573
I trust that Penny cares about me
and nothing's gonna happen with this guy.

129
00:08:10,782 --> 00:08:13,952
Unless of course he's a skilled hypnotist.

130
00:08:15,495 --> 00:08:16,663
What?

131
00:08:17,288 --> 00:08:19,958
While unlikely,
it's still a statistical possibility.

132
00:08:20,166 --> 00:08:25,255
She might be performing sexual acts
with him and not even know it.

133
00:08:27,173 --> 00:08:29,676
-Now you're just being ridiculous.
-Am I?

134
00:08:29,884 --> 00:08:32,637
The mind's a mysterious thing, Leonard.

135
00:08:32,846 --> 00:08:34,764
He could be having the time of his life...

136
00:08:34,973 --> 00:08:37,684
...while she thinks she's a chicken
pecking for corn.

137
00:08:48,778 --> 00:08:54,784
Look at us, sneaking around in the middle
of the night like a couple of cat burglars.

138
00:08:55,702 --> 00:08:57,120
I think we're more like ninjas.

139
00:08:57,328 --> 00:09:00,373
I don't wanna be a ninja.
I wanna be a cat burglar.

140
00:09:01,207 --> 00:09:05,795
-Fine. I'll be a ninja, you be a cat burglar.
-No, we both have to be the same thing.

141
00:09:09,382 --> 00:09:10,550
Okay, we're ninjas.

142
00:09:11,342 --> 00:09:13,053
Thank you.

143
00:09:13,553 --> 00:09:16,097
But next time we'll be cat burglars.

144
00:09:21,895 --> 00:09:23,229
-It's locked.
-Are you sure?

145
00:09:24,064 --> 00:09:25,690
Yes, I'm sure.

146
00:09:25,899 --> 00:09:28,401
Now, you stand guard, I'm--

147
00:09:31,738 --> 00:09:33,406
It's locked.

148
00:09:34,574 --> 00:09:38,244
Just keep an eye out.
I'll have this open in a minute.

149
00:09:38,453 --> 00:09:40,205
When did you learn how to pick locks?

150
00:09:40,413 --> 00:09:43,833
When I was starting to do magic
in junior high...

151
00:09:44,042 --> 00:09:47,253
...I thought I could be an escape artist
like Harry Houdini.

152
00:09:47,462 --> 00:09:49,214
-How did that work out?
-Pretty good.

153
00:09:49,422 --> 00:09:54,552
I managed to escape friends, popularity,
and every party thrown in a 12-mile radius.

154
00:09:55,553 --> 00:09:57,639
-There. Ready?
-Hold on, hold on.

155
00:09:57,847 --> 00:09:58,848
What?

156
00:09:59,057 --> 00:10:02,852
Sheldon is a very smart man, and
he obviously wants to keep this a secret.

157
00:10:03,061 --> 00:10:04,562
Yeah, so?

158
00:10:04,771 --> 00:10:07,607
What if it's booby-trapped?

159
00:10:07,941 --> 00:10:10,568
Don't worry, I'm one step ahead of him.

160
00:10:10,777 --> 00:10:13,154
Oh, great, what's your pl--?

161
00:10:18,284 --> 00:10:19,953
Are we good?

162
00:10:20,411 --> 00:10:21,454
Yeah.

163
00:10:28,586 --> 00:10:29,921
Huh.

164
00:10:30,630 --> 00:10:32,924
Forty-three.

165
00:10:33,133 --> 00:10:34,551
What the hell does that mean?

166
00:10:34,759 --> 00:10:36,219
I don't know.

167
00:10:36,427 --> 00:10:39,764
-The solution to an equation?
-Maybe.

168
00:10:39,973 --> 00:10:41,766
It's a prime number.

169
00:10:41,975 --> 00:10:44,811
Encryption systems are built
on prime numbers.

170
00:10:45,019 --> 00:10:48,481
What kind of secret
does Sheldon have to encrypt?

171
00:10:48,690 --> 00:10:53,319
He's always been very cagey about what
he puts in his egg salad to make it so tasty.

172
00:10:57,448 --> 00:10:59,325
It's paprika.

173
00:10:59,534 --> 00:11:00,785
Really?

174
00:11:00,994 --> 00:11:03,830
Well, okay, one mystery solved.

175
00:11:06,916 --> 00:11:09,460
COLE: Okay, good night.
PENNY: Thanks, Cole. See you at school.

176
00:11:09,669 --> 00:11:11,129
See you.

177
00:11:13,173 --> 00:11:14,757
I'll be right back.

178
00:11:14,966 --> 00:11:17,177
I thought you left a long time ago.

179
00:11:27,187 --> 00:11:28,687
What's up?

180
00:11:30,523 --> 00:11:32,442
Hey.

181
00:11:32,650 --> 00:11:35,320
You, uh, moving into the apartment
on the fifth floor?

182
00:11:35,528 --> 00:11:37,822
No. I was just visiting a friend.

183
00:11:38,031 --> 00:11:39,032
Oh, cool.

184
00:11:39,240 --> 00:11:41,743
-That cute blond on 4?
-Yeah. You know her?

185
00:11:41,951 --> 00:11:44,495
Oh, well, I see her around.

186
00:11:44,703 --> 00:11:49,708
I like to keep my distance
because her boyfriend's a pretty scary dude.

187
00:11:50,877 --> 00:11:52,754
-Really?
-Yeah.

188
00:11:52,962 --> 00:11:55,006
He's ganged up.

189
00:11:56,341 --> 00:11:58,176
She told me he's a scientist.

190
00:11:58,843 --> 00:12:01,471
That's the name of his gang.

191
00:12:03,890 --> 00:12:06,226
The Scientists.

192
00:12:06,976 --> 00:12:09,229
They are crazy.

193
00:12:10,021 --> 00:12:13,900
-Well, thanks for the tip.
-No problem, brother. Stay frosty.

194
00:12:26,496 --> 00:12:28,039
We're still dating, right?

195
00:12:35,171 --> 00:12:37,090
-Oh, hello, Dr. Hofstadter.
-Oh, hey, Alex.

196
00:12:37,298 --> 00:12:39,717
And call me Leonard.
Dr. Hofstadter is my father.

197
00:12:39,926 --> 00:12:41,094
And my mother.

198
00:12:42,761 --> 00:12:44,430
And my sister.

199
00:12:44,639 --> 00:12:46,432
And our cat.

200
00:12:47,934 --> 00:12:52,647
Although, I'm pretty sure Dr. Boots
Hofstadter's degree was honorary.

201
00:12:54,148 --> 00:12:55,608
-May I join you, Leonard?
-Sure.

202
00:12:55,817 --> 00:12:57,193
Thanks.

203
00:12:57,986 --> 00:12:59,654
Hey, Alex, let me ask you something.

204
00:13:00,863 --> 00:13:04,409
My girlfriend knows this guy at school.
He's got an English accent--

205
00:13:04,617 --> 00:13:06,369
Ooh, I love English accents.

206
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:08,955
Yeah, you all do.

207
00:13:09,956 --> 00:13:12,125
Anyway, I feel like he's hitting on her.

208
00:13:12,333 --> 00:13:14,919
She says he's just being nice
and I should trust her.

209
00:13:15,128 --> 00:13:16,921
It's probably harmless.

210
00:13:17,130 --> 00:13:20,591
You know how it is.
I'm sure you get hit on all the time.

211
00:13:22,468 --> 00:13:24,304
Right.

212
00:13:25,138 --> 00:13:28,433
Because girls are always like,
"Ooh, that guy owns two Star Trek uniforms...

213
00:13:28,641 --> 00:13:32,645
...and gets a lot of ear infections.
I gotta get me some of that."

214
00:13:34,188 --> 00:13:36,482
I don't know.
Bet it happens more than you realize.

215
00:13:36,691 --> 00:13:37,942
Trust me, it doesn't.

216
00:13:38,151 --> 00:13:39,444
You sure?

217
00:13:39,652 --> 00:13:41,321
You're cute, you're funny.

218
00:13:41,529 --> 00:13:44,365
Maybe you're getting hit on
and you don't even know it.

219
00:13:46,825 --> 00:13:48,119
Really?

220
00:13:48,328 --> 00:13:50,997
I'm pretty sure.

221
00:13:53,916 --> 00:13:55,960
[LAUGHING]

222
00:13:57,337 --> 00:14:00,673
Okay, I gotta get back to work.
Thanks for listening.

223
00:14:00,882 --> 00:14:05,678
-No problem.
-Hope no girls rip my clothes off on the way.

224
00:14:08,431 --> 00:14:11,476
Come on, we're smart guys.

225
00:14:11,684 --> 00:14:13,852
-We can figure this out.
-Forty-three.

226
00:14:14,062 --> 00:14:16,147
What is 43?

227
00:14:16,356 --> 00:14:19,776
Besides my mom's neck size.

228
00:14:22,195 --> 00:14:24,364
It's the atomic number for technetium.

229
00:14:25,239 --> 00:14:26,657
That stuff's radioactive.

230
00:14:27,241 --> 00:14:28,993
Do you think he's building a bomb?

231
00:14:29,202 --> 00:14:33,456
It took him two years to put together
that Lego Death Star. I'm not worried.

232
00:14:35,625 --> 00:14:37,001
Ooh, this could be something.

233
00:14:37,210 --> 00:14:43,049
Forty-three is the number of calories
in half a cup of fat-free yogurt.

234
00:14:44,384 --> 00:14:45,510
Why would you know that?

235
00:14:45,718 --> 00:14:50,390
I'm sorry, we can't all eat whatever we want
and still stay thin.

236
00:14:54,394 --> 00:14:56,646
Wait. In The Hitchhikers Guide
to the Galaxy...

237
00:14:56,854 --> 00:15:00,400
...isn't 43 the answer to the question of life,
the universe, and everything?

238
00:15:00,608 --> 00:15:03,403
That's 42, dumb-ass.

239
00:15:04,278 --> 00:15:05,363
Hey, hey.

240
00:15:05,571 --> 00:15:07,198
Feelings.

241
00:15:08,908 --> 00:15:10,910
We should stop. I'm exhausted.

242
00:15:11,119 --> 00:15:13,496
Yeah, me too.
We've got more important things to do.

243
00:15:13,704 --> 00:15:15,206
Who cares what stupid 43 means?

244
00:15:15,415 --> 00:15:17,291
-Not me.
-Let's go home.

245
00:15:20,086 --> 00:15:23,089
-Want to get something to eat?
-Sounds good.

246
00:15:25,258 --> 00:15:28,719
-What the balls is 43?
-I have to know.

247
00:15:34,100 --> 00:15:35,143
Hi.

248
00:15:35,351 --> 00:15:37,437
Hey. Shouldn't you be out with your gang...

249
00:15:37,645 --> 00:15:40,939
...spray-painting equations
on the side of buildings?

250
00:15:42,233 --> 00:15:45,069
-Come on, I'm sorry.
-I just can't believe you don't trust me.

251
00:15:45,278 --> 00:15:46,737
No, I do. Of course I do.

252
00:15:46,945 --> 00:15:51,075
Then why did you embarrass me in front of
my friend? Who knew exactly who you were.

253
00:15:51,284 --> 00:15:54,537
-Really?
-Your picture's on my refrigerator.

254
00:15:58,291 --> 00:16:01,294
You know, I'm really starting
to not like this guy.

255
00:16:02,128 --> 00:16:03,129
What is your problem?

256
00:16:03,337 --> 00:16:06,841
Do you use up your thinking at work
and have none left for home?

257
00:16:07,049 --> 00:16:08,426
I don't know.

258
00:16:08,634 --> 00:16:10,969
It's hard sometimes.

259
00:16:11,179 --> 00:16:12,972
Everywhere you go, guys hit on you.

260
00:16:13,181 --> 00:16:15,183
Even if I'm standing right there.

261
00:16:15,391 --> 00:16:19,061
And they're all taller than me.
Why is everyone taller than me?

262
00:16:19,978 --> 00:16:22,315
You know what, this is all in my head.

263
00:16:22,523 --> 00:16:23,941
It's my problem, not yours.

264
00:16:24,150 --> 00:16:25,818
Leonard, why do you always do this?

265
00:16:26,027 --> 00:16:28,821
Listen to me. You are the one I'm with.
You know I love you.

266
00:16:29,030 --> 00:16:31,699
So will you please relax
because you're driving me crazy.

267
00:16:38,664 --> 00:16:41,709
You know, that's the first time you ever said
that you love me.

268
00:16:43,586 --> 00:16:45,046
Yeah.

269
00:16:49,007 --> 00:16:51,594
We're just supposed to pretend
it's not a big deal?

270
00:16:53,888 --> 00:16:58,518
That's exactly what we're gonna do...

271
00:16:58,726 --> 00:17:02,230
...because you're about to make me cry.

272
00:17:02,438 --> 00:17:07,360
And we both know if I start crying
you're gonna start crying.

273
00:17:10,196 --> 00:17:12,448
-You're right, you should go.
-All right.

274
00:17:20,414 --> 00:17:22,208
She loves me.

275
00:17:22,917 --> 00:17:24,252
[PHONE BEEPS]

276
00:17:27,338 --> 00:17:29,382
"Hey, it's Alex.
Nice having coffee with you.

277
00:17:29,590 --> 00:17:34,011
If you want to talk more, I'm always available."
Smiley face, smiley face.

278
00:17:34,220 --> 00:17:36,389
What a friendly girl.

279
00:17:40,935 --> 00:17:44,146
Okay, picture's up.
Looks like the camera's working.

280
00:17:44,355 --> 00:17:45,731
That's good-quality video.

281
00:17:45,940 --> 00:17:49,151
It better be.
It's the spare camera for the Mars Rover.

282
00:17:50,361 --> 00:17:51,988
How did you get your hands on that?

283
00:17:52,196 --> 00:17:54,991
Million-dollar camera. Ten-dollar lock.

284
00:17:56,701 --> 00:17:59,870
Oh, my God, here he comes. This is it.

285
00:18:08,713 --> 00:18:11,173
-What the hell is that thing?
-I don't know.

286
00:18:11,382 --> 00:18:12,842
SHELDON:
This is Dr. Sheldon Cooper.

287
00:18:13,050 --> 00:18:15,511
Experimental log.

288
00:18:15,720 --> 00:18:19,515
Wormhole generator, test 44.

289
00:18:19,724 --> 00:18:22,476
Wormhole-generator test?

290
00:18:25,730 --> 00:18:26,772
[BOTH GASP]

291
00:18:27,356 --> 00:18:32,486
The first 43 parallel universes I've checked
proved to be empty.

292
00:18:32,695 --> 00:18:37,575
I see no reason to suspect universe
number 44 will be any different.

293
00:18:43,706 --> 00:18:47,209
-Oh, my God!
-Holy crap!

294
00:18:50,671 --> 00:18:52,632
[SHELDON SCREAMING ON-SCREEN]

295
00:18:52,840 --> 00:18:55,509
Ow! It's eating my face!

296
00:18:56,052 --> 00:18:57,178
It's eating his face!

297
00:18:57,386 --> 00:18:58,846
[BOTH SCREAM]

298
00:19:04,477 --> 00:19:06,687
I found your webcam
and replaced the video feed.

299
00:19:06,896 --> 00:19:09,023
You two should be ashamed of yourselves.

300
00:19:09,231 --> 00:19:12,026
-Sheldon, we're really sorry.
-Yeah, really sorry.

301
00:19:12,234 --> 00:19:13,277
Sorry?

302
00:19:13,486 --> 00:19:18,199
You may not realize it, but I have difficulty
navigating certain aspects of daily life.

303
00:19:18,407 --> 00:19:22,578
You know, understanding sarcasm.
Feigning interest in others.

304
00:19:24,080 --> 00:19:26,415
Not talking about trains
as much as I want to.

305
00:19:27,124 --> 00:19:28,167
It's exhausting.

306
00:19:28,376 --> 00:19:32,380
Which is why, for 20 minutes a day,
I like to go down to that room...

307
00:19:32,588 --> 00:19:36,133
...turn my mind off,
and do what I need to do to recharge.

308
00:19:37,009 --> 00:19:39,220
But what are you doing in there?

309
00:19:39,428 --> 00:19:40,846
And what does 43 mean?

310
00:19:41,055 --> 00:19:43,724
You don't need to know,
you don't deserve to know...

311
00:19:43,933 --> 00:19:46,268
...and you will never know.

312
00:19:47,311 --> 00:19:50,523
Yeah, well, I know how to make
your egg salad now.

313
00:20:31,021 --> 00:20:33,566
One, two, three, four...

314
00:20:33,774 --> 00:20:40,114
...five, six, seven, eight-- Drat.

315
00:20:42,450 --> 00:20:45,578
I'm never gonna get to 43 again.

316
00:20:45,786 --> 00:20:51,167
One, two, three, four-- Rats.

